@using Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Blazor
@using Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Contracts
@inject NavigationManager Nav

@* The handover out of Kelda, in the one shape that actually works.

   This wrapper exists for a single reason, and it is worth stating plainly because the arrangement
   it replaces looked correct and was not. An EventCallback does not survive being passed from a
   statically-rendered parent into an interactive island. It is not rejected either — Blazor throws
   for a bare delegate parameter, but EventCallback is a struct, so it is serialised as
   {"HasDelegate":true} and read back inside the circuit as an empty delegate. The handler then
   never runs, and KildeExplorer, which draws its selection column only where the handover can
   actually happen, correctly draws nothing.

   `@rendermode` on the component tag does not fix that: it makes the MOUNT POINT interactive while
   the parent that creates the callback is still static. What fixes it is the callback being created
   inside the interactive component — so the wrapper is the island, and ExploreVariables below is
   its own method.

   That is the same shape LegacyHost's KildeExplorerWithUrlState has, and now for the same reason
   rather than by coincidence: helsedata's Optimizely host mounts a wrapper too. Keeping the page
   itself static also keeps the notes above the frame rendering on the first byte.

   The front page mounts VariableExplorer straight onto the tag with @rendermode, and that stays
   right: it passes Filter, which is data, and data serialises across the boundary intact. *@

<KildeExplorer Language="@Language"
               ExploreVariablesRequested="ExploreVariables" />

@code {
    [Parameter] public string Language { get; set; } = "no";

    /// <summary>
    /// Turn the reader's chosen kilder into the query the variable explorer already reads, and go
    /// there.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The host's job, because the host is the only side that knows where it mounted a
    /// <c>VariableExplorer</c> — the package has no router and never touches the address bar. The
    /// format is not restated here either: <c>VariableFilter.ToQueryString</c> writes exactly what
    /// <c>VariableFilter.Parse</c> reads on the front page, so the two ends cannot drift apart.
    /// <para>
    /// An empty list is not a selection of none. It is what the component sends when the reader
    /// narrowed nothing, so it lands on the unfiltered variable list rather than on a query asking
    /// for no kilder at all.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// An ordinary in-app navigation, unlike LegacyHost's, which has to force a load because its
    /// front page is an MVC view with no router behind it. The component is the same in both; what
    /// differs is the hosting model, which is why both samples are kept.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private void ExploreVariables(IReadOnlyList<Guid> kildeIds)
    {
        var query = new VariableFilter { KildeIds = kildeIds }.ToQueryString();

        Nav.NavigateTo(query.Length == 0 ? "/" : "/?" + query);
    }
}
